 "finished_by_agent": true,
 "finish_summary": "Optimized priority32 by eliminating the redundant 32-input OR-reduction (hit_0) that duplicated information already implicit in the priority chain. Instead of separately computing hit_0 = |a_i and idx_0 via a ternary chain then combining as {hit_0, idx_0}, the winning version directly encodes the combined 6-bit result (32+bit_index, or 0 if none) in a single priority ternary chain, so the \"found\" flag falls out naturally from whether the chain result is nonzero. This was formally proven equivalent (Yosys equiv_induct -seq 4: PROVEN) and passed the full simulation benchmark. Explored alternatives included a full balanced binary tree leading-one-detector (formally proven, but place-and-route score 76.114, worse than baseline), a for-loop-based combinational priority encoder, and a two-level (16+16) hybrid chain (P&R score 76.440). The best verified result was the single folded-flag priority chain: post-route area 205.352 um^2, WNS 7.6883 ns, score 76.677648, versus the reference RTL's 208.81 um^2 / 7.64557 ns / 76.24689 \u2014 an improvement of about +0.43 in score with lower area and better timing margin, at zero behavioral difference (cycle-accurate equivalence proven).",
